---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import { guideBySlug } from '../../data/site';
import { CURRENT_TAX_YEAR as YEAR } from '../../data/tax-years';
import { calculateTerminationTax } from '../../lib/redundancy';

const guide = guideBySlug('is-redundancy-pay-taxable');
const r = YEAR.redundancy;
const gbp = (n: number) => `£${Math.round(n).toLocaleString('en-GB')}`;

// A realistic package, worked through the same engine as the calculator.
const example = calculateTerminationTax(
  {
    redundancyPay: 28_000,
    payInLieuOfNotice: 9_000,
    holidayPay: 1_500,
    otherIncomeThisTaxYear: 30_000,
  },
  YEAR,
);
---

<GuideLayout guide={guide} sourceKey="redundancy">
  <p class="tool-standfirst">
    The first {gbp(r.terminationPaymentExemption)} of redundancy pay is tax free — but that
    exemption is narrower than almost everyone assumes, and notice pay falls entirely outside it.
  </p>

  <h2>The short answer</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Redundancy pay</strong> — first {gbp(r.terminationPaymentExemption)} free of income
      tax and National Insurance. Above that, income tax applies but National Insurance still does
      not.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Pay in lieu of notice</strong> — fully taxable, with National Insurance. No part of
      the exemption applies.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Holiday pay</strong> — fully taxable, with National Insurance.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Contractual bonus owed</strong> — fully taxable, with National Insurance.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Three things people get wrong</h2>

  <h3>1. It covers the redundancy payment, not the package</h3>
  <p>
    Employers frequently present an offer as "£30,000 tax free". Usually it is not. The exemption
    applies only to genuine termination payments — statutory redundancy pay plus any enhanced or
    ex-gratia element. Everything else in the package is ordinary earnings.
  </p>

  <h3>2. It is one {gbp(r.terminationPaymentExemption)} in total</h3>
  <p>
    Not per payment, not per element, and not renewed if payments straddle two tax years. It is a
    single {gbp(r.terminationPaymentExemption)} across everything arising from the same employment.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Notice pay is taxable however it is labelled</h3>
  <p>
    Since April 2018 the post-employment notice pay rules mean payment in lieu of notice is taxed as
    earnings regardless of what your contract says or how the payment is described. The old
    distinction between contractual and non-contractual PILON no longer helps.
  </p>

  <h2>A worked example</h2>
  <p>
    Someone made redundant in October, having earned {gbp(30_000)} so far that tax year, receiving:
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <caption>A {gbp(example.totalPackage)} package</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Element</th>
          <th scope="col">Amount</th>
          <th scope="col">Treatment</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Redundancy pay</td>
          <td>{gbp(28_000)}</td>
          <td style="white-space:normal;text-align:right">Within the exemption — tax free</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Pay in lieu of notice</td>
          <td>{gbp(9_000)}</td>
          <td style="white-space:normal;text-align:right">Fully taxable, plus NI</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Holiday pay</td>
          <td>{gbp(1_500)}</td>
          <td style="white-space:normal;text-align:right">Fully taxable, plus NI</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
      <tfoot>
        <tr>
          <td>Income tax</td>
          <td>{gbp(example.incomeTax)}</td>
          <td></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>National Insurance</td>
          <td>{gbp(example.nationalInsurance)}</td>
          <td></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Actually received</td>
          <td>{gbp(example.netPackage)}</td>
          <td></td>
        </tr>
      </tfoot>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    {gbp(example.incomeTax + example.nationalInsurance)} of tax on a package presented as largely
    tax free — all of it on the {gbp(10_500)} of notice and holiday pay.
    <a href="/redundancy-pay-calculator/">Work out your own package</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>How to reduce the tax</h2>

  <h3>Pay it into a pension</h3>
  <p>
    The most effective option by far. An employer contribution paid directly from the package
    escapes income tax and National Insurance entirely — including on the taxable elements. Watch
    the {gbp(YEAR.pensions.annualAllowance)} annual allowance, and check your scheme will accept a
    contribution once employment has ended.
  </p>

  <h3>Consider the timing</h3>
  <p>
    The package is taxed in the year you receive it, stacked on income already earned. Being made
    redundant late in a tax year, when your allowance and basic rate band are used up, pushes more
    into higher rate. Where there is any flexibility about the leaving date, model both sides of 6
    April.
  </p>

  <h3>Check your tax code afterwards</h3>
  <p>
    Employers often apply an emergency code to a final payment, which frequently overtaxes it. If
    you do not start a new job immediately you can reclaim using form P50, or P53 for a lump sum,
    rather than waiting until the year end.
  </p>

  <h2>What about the redundancy itself?</h2>
  <p>
    Statutory redundancy pay for {YEAR.label} uses a weekly pay cap of {gbp(r.weeklyPayCap)} —{' '}
    {gbp(r.weeklyPayCapNorthernIreland)} in Northern Ireland — giving a statutory maximum of{' '}
    {gbp(r.weeklyPayCap * r.maximumYears * r.weeksPerYear41Plus)}. Many employers pay more under a
    contractual scheme, so check your contract and staff handbook.
    <a href="/redundancy-pay-calculator/">Calculate your entitlement</a>.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <strong>Take advice on a settlement agreement</strong>
    <p>
      If you are offered one, you must take independent legal advice for it to be binding — and the
      employer normally pays for it. Use that advice. It is the point at which the numbers can still
      change, and Acas offers free guidance on the redundancy process itself.
    </p>
  </div>
</GuideLayout>
